grund fmt — normalize citation syntax: rewrite the `$$` trigger to the `§` marker,
optionally upgrade bare ID tokens, and optionally emit Markdown cross-reference links.

After a declaration rename or file move, first edit the declaration and affected
authored citation IDs. When cross-reference emission applies, fmt re-derives generated
Markdown link destinations and anchors; it does not repair arbitrary URLs or rename IDs.

Run --write from the config/project root or workspace root to update eligible section locks.
Locks are keyed by declaration ID: a file move alone does not rename a lock file.
fmt never silently approves a protected section move; fix citations or leave a pointer first.
Then run grund check and grund fmt --check, and commit the generated lock changes.

Usage:  grund fmt [PATH] [--check | --write] [--marker] [--cross-refs]

With no PATH, the run covers the whole project or workspace discovered from the current directory; a PATH narrows it.

Options:
  --check        report pending rewrites, exit 1 if any exist         e.g. grund fmt --check
  --write        apply the changes in place                           e.g. grund fmt --write
  --marker       also prefix bare `<ID>` tokens with the marker        e.g. grund fmt --write --marker
  --cross-refs   wrap citations as Markdown links to targets          e.g. grund fmt --write --cross-refs
                 runs by default in both modes for Markdown scopes; in version 1, set [fmt.cross_refs].enabled = false to opt out
  --path-base project|invocation
                       project (default) spells report paths from the config root;
                       invocation: relative to the path you passed, or the current directory if you passed none.
                       e.g. grund fmt docs/ --path-base=invocation
                       --path-base=invocation replaces a version-1 `[output] relative_paths = false`.

With neither --check nor --write, fmt previews every change --write would apply and exits 1 if any.
Each preview row names every kind of rewrite its line gets: `bare → marker, markdown link`.
--write prints `rewrote N lines:` and its totals, e.g. `rewrote 7 lines: 6 citations marked, 6 links written`,
then one `  <path> (count)` line per file touched.
After a rename, a lock file fmt deletes or creates says so: its first preview row ends
`; deletes the file` or `; creates the file`, and its --write line ends ` deleted` or ` created`.
The report goes to stdout (like `grund check`); CLI-level `error:` lines go to stderr.

Exit:  0 nothing to do, or --write succeeded · 1 changes pending (dry run / --check) · 2 unreadable tree or CLI error.

Examples:
  grund fmt                       # preview every rewrite --write would apply
  grund fmt --check               # the same preview as a gate: exit 1 if pending
  grund fmt --write               # after an authored rename/move, maintain links and locks from the root
  grund fmt --write --marker      # also mark bare ID tokens
